-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Intractability:** Computing the full Hessian $H$ is intractable for large models. FisherRF handles this by keeping only the diagonal elements [S1].
-*   **Sparsity:** Matrices are highly sparse due to the disentangling of scene parameters with respect to disparate rays. This sparsity allows for cost-effective computation of the matrix trace (if used) or diagonal updates [S2].
-*   **Independence from Ground Truth:** The Hessian of the objective function in volumetric rendering is independent of ground truth data or actual image measurements. This allows information gain to be computed using only camera parameters of candidate views [S2].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The computation of the diagonal Hessian is as cost-effective as back-propagation [S2].
-*   **Performance:** When using 3D Gaussian Splatting, the method evaluates views at 70 fps [S2].
-*   **Comparison to Alternatives:** Compared to Goli et al., which relies on a hypothetical perturbation field and multiple function calls to the PyTorch backward engine, FisherRF's efficient CUDA implementation for diagonal Hessian computation takes $11.3 \text{ ms} \pm 33.3 \text{ \mu s}$ versus $1.1 \text{ s} \pm 2.5 \text{ ms}$ [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Direct quantification of observed information on model parameters; efficient active view selection; supports batch and path selection [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the diagonal approximation of the Hessian, which ignores parameter correlations [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The computation of the Hessian is additive over rays [S2]. Due to the sparsity of the scene parameters with respect to disparate rays, the matrix trace computation is as cost-effective as back-propagation [S2].
+*   **Performance:** When implemented with 3D Gaussian Splatting, the method evaluates views at 70 fps [S2]. A direct comparison shows FisherRF's efficient CUDA implementation for diagonal Hessian computation takes $11.3 \text{ ms} \pm 33.3 \text{ \mu s}$, compared to $1.1 \text{ s} \pm 2.5 \text{ ms}$ for methods relying on multiple PyTorch backward engine calls (e.g., Goli et al.) [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** By keeping only the diagonal elements of the Hessian, memory usage is reduced to $O(|\theta|)$, where $|\theta|$ is the number of parameters [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Direct quantification of observed information on parameters; efficient active view selection and mapping; state-of-the-art results in uncertainty quantification [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the diagonal approximation of the Hessian, ignoring parameter correlations [S1]. Assumes the posterior is Gaussian (Laplace approximation) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-FisherRF was introduced to address the lack of literature on active "training" view selection for volumetric rendering. It leverages Fisher Information to directly quantify observed information on the parameters of Radiance Fields, selecting candidate views by maximizing Expected Information Gain (EIG) [S2]. The method is implemented on top of point-based 3D Gaussian Splatting and Plenoxels radiance field models [S2]. It is cited as the first method to quantify uncertainty directly on model parameters for Radiance Fields using an efficient formulation [S2].
+FisherRF was introduced to address the lack of literature on active "training" view selection for volumetric rendering [S2]. It leverages Fisher Information to directly quantify observed information on the parameters of Radiance Fields, selecting candidate views by maximizing Expected Information Gain (EIG) [S2]. The method is implemented on top of point-based 3D Gaussian Splatting and Plenoxels radiance field models [S2]. It improves upon previous approaches that relied on hypothetical perturbation fields and indirect approximations, which did not guarantee optimal information gain [S2]. The Hessian of the objective function in volumetric rendering is independent of ground truth data or actual image measurements, allowing information gain to be computed using only camera parameters of candidate views [S2].
 
